# Snyk (https://snyk.io) policy file.
#
# `exclude.code` keeps test code out of Snyk Code: the fixtures in
# these paths hold throwaway passwords and tokens on purpose, and they buried every
# other finding (REB-463). A false positive in shipped code is not excluded here: its
# ignore belongs in Snyk's own UI, since Snyk Code reads no ignore from this file or from
# an inline comment, and a comment above the flagged line says why it is safe. Developer
# scripts (`tooling/`, `shared/brand/tools/`, `projects/*/scripts/`) stay scanned: an
# exclusion would hide their next real finding along with today's false one.
#
# `ignore` holds what the weekly container scan (`snyk container test`, passed
# `--policy-path=.snyk` in `.github/workflows/snyk-weekly.yml`) does not report: a Debian
# package with no fixed version yet, ignored with its reason (REB-540). An ignore made in
# Snyk's UI does not reach that scan, which is not a monitored project, so it is written
# here instead. Each entry expires, so a patch is looked for again: keys are Snyk ids,
# path `'*'` is every occurrence, and `expires` is `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss.fffZ`.
version: v1.25.1
ignore:
  SNYK-DEBIAN13-ACL-17677866:
    - '*':
        reason: "No fix yet (REB-540). Debian's security tracker lists CVE-2026-54369 (acl) as vulnerable for trixie on 2026-10-06, and a fresh python:3.13-slim-trixie has no newer libacl1 to install. Look again when Debian ships a fix."
        expires: '2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z'
  SNYK-DEBIAN13-ACL-17677882:
    - '*':
        reason: "No fix yet (REB-540). Debian's security tracker lists CVE-2026-54370 (acl) as vulnerable for trixie on 2026-10-06, and a fresh python:3.13-slim-trixie has no newer libacl1 to install. Look again when Debian ships a fix."
